{"name":"Divine White","hex":"#e6dccd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"divine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to a god.","Eternal, holy, or otherwise godlike."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One skilled in divinity; a theologian.","A minister of the gospel; a priest; a clergyman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/divine"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Divinity","blocks":[{"text":"Divinity refers to the quality, presence, or nature of that which is divine—a term that, before the rise of monotheism, evoked a broad and dynamic field of sacred power. In the ancient world, divinity was not limited to a single deity or abstract ideal but was recognized in multiple forms: as a radiant attribute possessed by gods, as a vital force cushioning nature, and even as a quality glimpsed in extraordinary humans, laws, or acts. The Latin divinitas and its Greek counterparts conveyed something both immanent and awe-inspiring: a presence that could be felt in thunder, justice, ecstasy, fate, or beauty."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Isaac_Oliver_Elizabeth_I_and_the_Three_Goddesses.jpg/330px-Isaac_Oliver_Elizabeth_I_and_the_Three_Goddesses.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divinity"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg/500px-Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg/500px-Alexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Transfiguration by Alexandr Ivanov, 1824","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlexandr_Ivanov_015.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg/500px-Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg/1280px-Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg/500px-Hildegard_von_Bingen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hildegard von Bingen receives a divine inspiration and passes it on to her scribe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHildegard_von_Bingen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/The_Cloud_of_Unknowing_%28Harley_2373_%29.jpg/500px-The_Cloud_of_Unknowing_%28Harley_2373_%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia